# Dockyard configuration — copy to `.env` and edit, then `docker compose up -d`.
# Every value below is OPTIONAL; the defaults shown are what the app uses when a
# variable is unset. Docker Compose reads this file automatically.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Network exposure
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which host interface the web UI binds to.
#   127.0.0.1 (default) → reachable ONLY from this machine (http://localhost).
#   0.0.0.0             → reachable from other devices on your LAN.
#
# ⚠  Dockyard controls the Docker daemon, so it is root-equivalent on this host.
#    Only expose it on a network you trust, and never directly to the internet —
#    put a hardened reverse proxy + real TLS + your own auth in front instead.
# BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1

# Host ports for the web UI (the container always serves 80/443 internally;
# these are the HOST ports you browse to). The defaults are deliberately uncommon
# so they don't clash with anything already on 80/443 — browse to
# http://localhost:9272 (or https://localhost:9273). Set these to 80/443 if you
# do want the standard ports.
# HTTP_PORT=9272
# HTTPS_PORT=9273

# Host port for the bundled image registry (always bound to 127.0.0.1). Default
# is uncommon to avoid the frequently-taken 5000.
# REGISTRY_PORT=5273

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pre-built images (CI / GHCR)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which image tag to run when consuming the images CI publishes to GHCR instead
# of building locally. `latest` tracks the main branch; pin a release such as
# 0.0.1 for a reproducible deploy. To update:
#   docker compose pull && docker compose up -d
#
# The packages are published to GHCR. Make each package PUBLIC (recommended — the
# images hold no secrets) so pulls and in-app update checks work with no login; or
# keep them private and log in once on this host with a read:packages token:
#   echo <TOKEN> | docker login ghcr.io -u <github-user> --password-stdin
# DOCKYARD_TAG=latest

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Secrets at rest
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AES-256 key (base64, exactly 32 bytes) that encrypts git tokens and stack
# secrets in the database. If left empty, a key is generated on first run and
# stored at /data/secret.key INSIDE the backend-data volume — i.e. next to the
# data it protects. Losing that volume then makes those secrets unrecoverable.
#
# RECOMMENDED for anything you care about: generate a key here and store a copy
# somewhere safe (a password manager), so it is backed up independently:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# DOCKYARD_SECRET_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CORS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated allowed origins. Empty → "*", which is safe here because auth
# is bearer-token (no ambient cookies). Set this only if you serve the UI from a
# different origin than the API.
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Events / audit log
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How many days of events/audit history to keep (older rows are pruned hourly).
# A busy host generates a lot of daemon events, so lower this to cap table growth.
# EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Volume backups
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How many backups to keep per volume (newest N; older ones are pruned).
# BACKUP_KEEP=10

# How often (seconds) the opt-in scheduler checks for due backups. A schedule
# still fires at most once per its own configured interval.
# BACKUP_SCHEDULE_TICK_SECONDS=300

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Application backups (Dockyard's own DB + secrets + stacks/repos/projects)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HOST directory where application backups are written. Defaults to ./backups
# next to this compose file. Point it at an external disk / NAS mount for true
# off-host durability (these survive `docker volume rm`). The directory is taken
# over by the app's uid (10001) on first run, so use a dedicated path.
# BACKUP_HOST_DIR=./backups
